\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 Evaluate the extent to which global governance is more concerned with economic issues rather than human rights issues [30] \par
\par
 \par
\par
Global governance is the political integration (NOT REALLY THE RIGHT WORD \f1\endash  YOU'D DESCRIBED EU MEMBER STATES AS BEING INTEGRATED, BUT YOU WOULDN\rquote T DESCRIBED SECURITY COUNCIL P5 MEMBERS AS BEING INTEGRATED \endash  THINK ABOUT WHY) of transnational actors, especially nations. Economic issues include trade, poverty, and the development of nations in the historical global south. Human rights issues, on the other hand, include the persecution of offenders and the protection of those whose human rights are breached. Human rights are most commonly understood as the four freedoms being the right to freedom of speech, freedom of expression, freedom from want, and freedom from fear. Firstly, the long-established nature of economic supranational organisations such as the World Trade Organisation will be covered in contrast to the newly developed human rights organisations. Secondly, the difference in membership between economic and human rights organisations will be covered. Lastly, consensus over the corresponding issues and the concern it indicates will be explored. It will be concluded that economic issues do indeed receive more concern from the global community. THIS FINAL SECTION OF THE INTRO IS GREAT, REALLY CLEARLY SETTING OUT YOUR POINTS AND JUDGEMENT.  \par
\par
 \par
\par
It could be argued that the shift to the creation of human rights bodies could show that the global order has more concern for human rights THIS POINT SENTENCE SHOULD BRIEFLY MENTION ECONOMIC TOO, SO THEY KNOW YOU ARE FOCUSED ON THE WHOLE QUESTION. There have been several more recent developments in human rights governance that could show a shifting concern. For example, the International Criminal Court was founded in 2002 after being signed on by 120 countries. The ICC focuses on prosecuting individuals who have breached human rights, such as despots including Charles Taylor from Liberia. This more recent shift towards human rights could indicate a growing concern of the actors that make up global governance. This could suggest for governments that human rights have moved above areas of global governance, including economic. However, it can be more convincingly argued that the long-standing nature of economic institutions means that they have been a longstanding concern that continues to be addressed. Human rights bodies such as the aforementioned ICC and special tribunals have only been around since the 1990s, suggesting that the world order has been more focused on economic issues for a long time. There are a number of established economic institutions committed to the development of global trade and other issues. The Bretton Woods Institutions\emdash the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank\emdash were both founded in July 1944 and continue to be influential CAN YOU GIVE EXAMPLES OF THIS INFLUENCE? WASHINGTON CONSENSUS? NUMBER OF WORLD BANK PROJECTS? REACH OF WTO?. This clearly shows that economic issues are of particular importance to the global community and have long been seen this way. \par
\par
 \par
\par
It could be argued that human rights governance organisations have garnered a good amount of membership since their recent establishment. Human rights bodies have struggled to establish universal membership, but the ICC has managed to gain 125 members since their founding under the Rome Statute in 2002. There are several important states that have joined the ICC, such as the United Kingdom, and many areas are covered. However, it can be more convincingly argued that economic institutions with near-universal membership show a further concern with economic issues. The ICC lacks many key players, such as China, India, and the United States. These highly influential and populous countries have refused to join the ICC. Additionally, the courts 125 members out of 193 UN-recognised states is low compared to many economically focused institutions. Almost all members of the international community are members of the IMF, World Bank, and WTO. The IMF has 191 out of 193 UN members, the World Bank has 189, and the WTO has 166, covering 98% of world trade as of August 2024. Additionally, the G7 is full of highly influential countries, not missing the key players many human rights institutions do. Almost universal membership suggests that states are much more concerned with economic matters than they are with human rights issues. Additionally, the presence of many regional economic bodies suggests that even for smaller states it is a concern more than human rights issues, for example, ECOWAS in Africa. Generally, nations seem to be much more invested and concerned in the institutions focused on economic issues rather than human rights ones. GREAT SECTION \par
\par
 \par
\par
It could be argued that human rights are less likely to be a concern since finding consensus on the issue is highly difficult. Cultural differences mean that finding a standard definition for human rights and then enforcing them is incredibly difficult. This can be contrasted with economic issues, where there has been a general agreement between most countries since the Cold War. This is known as the Washington Consensus and has seen free trade and economic organisations such as the WTO and the IMF dominate the international stage. Several states, due to their differing views on human rights, go out of their way to ignore them, showing little concern. The United Nations P5 (permanent five security council members), China frequently goes out of their way to try and deny measures to protect Rohingya Muslims in Myanmar due to being more concerned over issues of trade with Myanmar. This would suggest that states generally show more concern for economic issues due to the easier unity and consensus over them. However, it can more convincingly be argued that there has been a growing consensus over human rights, contrasting the increasing divisions over economic governance. The increasing foundation of human rights organisations shows a growing concern, and the increasing membership shows further consensus. In contrast, realist leaders have started to give up free trade in favour of protectionist policies, indicating there is now less of a global consensus than there may have been in the past. For example, American president Donald Trump threatened the European Union with 25% tariffs on the 26th of February 2025, claiming that the EU was founded to \lquote screw the US.\rquote . Several states have moved away from the economic collaboration of the immediate post-Cold War period and abandoned the Bretton Woods institutions. There has been a loss of confidence in these organisations after the economic crash of 2007, pushing developing nations towards other states. This has resulted in China\rquote s Belt and Road Initiative. This fracturing of global economic consensus, driven by emerging leaders' more realist outlooks, suggests that there is now less of a global economic concern and more focus on individual states. This is strongly contrasted by the more recent emergence of human rights organisations, which are growing in popularity and membership. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Overall, it is most convincing to argue that global governance is clearly more concerned with economic issues rather than human rights ones. While it could be argued that the more recent emergence of human rights organisations suggests a growing concern, the longstanding nature of economic organisations suggests it has been a longer and still-standing priority for the global community. Further, it could be argued that human rights organisations have garnered impressive membership; this still is much smaller than the nigh-universal membership economic institutions (especially the Bretton Woods institutions) enjoy. Lastly, it could be argued that it is difficult to form consensus over human rights issues due to cultural differences; however, it is more convincing to argue that there has instead been a reduction in consensus over economic concerns. Overall, it is most convincing to argue there is more concern over economic issues than human rights ones from the global community. \par
